# Normalize line endings so a Windows checkout (Git's default
# `core.autocrlf=true`) cannot hand CRLF files to bash. A `\r` on the shebang
# or `set -euo pipefail` line breaks the build with
# `set: pipefail\r: invalid option name`.
#
# `eol=lf` (not just `text=auto`) is required: every blob in this repo is already
# LF, but `text=auto` alone lets `core.autocrlf=true` write CRLF into a Windows
# working tree. oxfmt enforces LF, so `bun run format:check` — and therefore
# `bun run check` — then fails on every file it checks on Windows while staying
# green on Linux/macOS CI. Pinning the worktree to LF keeps all three platforms
# byte-identical. The `*.bat`/`*.cmd`/`*.ps1` rules below are more specific and
# still win, so Windows-native scripts keep CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts run under Git Bash / WSL on Windows: must stay LF.
*.sh text eol=lf
.husky/commit-msg text eol=lf
.husky/pre-commit text eol=lf
.husky/pre-push text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts want CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.dylib binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.node binary
*.wasm binary
bun.lock -diff linguist-generated
